WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A new x-ray catalog of 2 million sources shows galaxy cluster outskirts hotter and cleaner than models predicted. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 160. The E-Rosita telescope surveyed half the sky and produced a catalog containing nearly 2 million x-ray sources, approximately double the size of its first release. A team at Bon examined the faintest outer regions of a massive galaxy cluster, where fresh gas continues to arrive along intergalactic

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.960
filaments. Measurements showed this gas to be hotter, denser, and poorer in heavy elements than current hydrodynamic models predicted. No earlier observation had reached this low surface brightness regime. The complete catalog is now publicly available for community analysis. The E-Rosita catalogs outer cluster measurement shows gas that is both hotter and less enriched than simulations predicted. The discrepancy appears only at the lowest surface brightness levels now reach a

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:29.880
stable for the first time. This region is where fresh intergalactic material continues to flow in along cosmic filaments. The findings suggests current models miss key physics governing how clusters assemble. Follow-up x-ray and Sunyaia-vzeldovich observations will test whether the pattern holds across more systems. Imagine a filament of gas stretching between two galaxy clusters that spans a distance greater than the drive from New York to Chicago, yet contains less mass per cubic meter

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:59.860
than the air inside a party balloon at cruising altitude. That is the physical scale of the material E-Rosita measured for the first time in its second data release. Fresh gas falling inward along these filaments should compress and heat according to standard hydrodynamic prescriptions. Yet the new observations record temperatures several million degrees higher, and heavy element abundances several times lower than any simulation currently produces. The telescope reached this regime by stacking thousands of faint photos

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:27.600
of photons collected across half the sky, pushing the surface brightness limit well below previous x-ray surveys. No one yet knows whether the excess heat originates from undetected shocks, from energy injected by earlier generations of galaxies, or from mixing processes that transport metals less efficiently than models assume. The single unanswered question is whether every massive cluster will display the same mismatch once its outskirts are examined at this depth.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:56.300
New catalogs and long baseline observations continue to refine our picture of how the universe builds and recycles its largest structures. From the unexpected physics at cluster edges, we turn to a mission that also pushes observational boundaries but on a commercial timeline. Virgin Galactic announced it will not begin commercial flights of its new suborbital space plane until early 2027. The delay of several months follows continued vehicle testing and certification efforts.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:19.020
No specific technical barriers were identified in the update. The revised schedule shifts the start from the previously targeted late 2026 window. Observers are monitoring the next series of test flights to assess whether the new timeline remains on track. While one operator adjusts its schedule, another space-based observatory is already delivering on its timeline.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:41.440
The media sat third-generation imager on MTG-I-1 recorded the moon's dark shadow crossing Greenland, Iceland, Northeastern Portugal and Spain. Images were acquired from geostationary orbit at 36,000 km altitude between 8.26 pm and 8.34 pm central European summertime on 12th August.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:51.660
The same data set also captured the partial eclipse visible from Northern Canada, the Nordic countries and Western Africa as far south as Sierra Leone and Liberia.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:04:01.500
MTG-I-2, the third satellite in the series is scheduled for launch on August 27, 2026 aboard an Ariane 6 from French Guiana.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.720
The mission continues building toward full operational coverage of Europe and Northern Africa.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:20.820
From operational satellites watching Earth, we moved to readers who traveled to experience the eclipse directly. A group of roughly 50 astronomy readers observed one minute and 30 seconds of totality from Madrid on the evening of 12 August.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:30.960
The travelers had spent the preceding week exploring the region before the eclipse. Participants described the sudden darkness in the visible solar corona as mesmerizing.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:40.900
The magazine published first-hand accounts along with images captured during the event. Planning is already underway for additional reader expeditions to future eclipses.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:55.060
Personal eclipse stories lead naturally to another long-term observational effort now bearing fruit. A Polish German collaboration tracked a single blazar continuously using two orbiting observatories over nearly 20 years.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:05:07.240
Two observations from 2012 revealed a spectral feature that no existing model had predicted. Conventional blazar campaigns typically span only a few days and focus on narrow energy bands.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:18.260
The extended baseline uncovered variability patterns that shorter campaigns had missed. Researchers are now examining other multi-year data sets for comparable unexpected features.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:35.920
Long baseline data continue to surprise us. Next, we look at how the earliest supernovae shaped the ingredients for planets. University of Portsmouth researchers calculate that the first supernovae enriched the cosmos with rocky planet building blocks only 100 million years after the Big Bang.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:45.020
This enrichment occurred well before the first galaxies assembled. The models follow how supernova eject a cooled and condensed into dust grains.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:54.580
Those grains later supplied the raw material for terrestrial planets. Next steps include searching for the predicted dust signatures within the oldest known galaxies.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:08.980
From the earliest building blocks, we turn to a dying star whose material is being recycled right now. Astronomers obtained images of a dying star whose ejected fragments are being broken apart and mixed into the surrounding interstellar medium.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:21.980
The observations capture the stripping and absorption process at a level of detail not previously achieved. Data reveal how raw materials from stellar death reenter the galactic reservoir for future star formation.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:33.920
The system lies thousands of light years away yet displays clear chemical signatures of ongoing dispersal. Follow-up spectroscopy is planned to map the spatial distribution of the dispersed elements.

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:46.680
Material cycling through galaxies brings us to a proposal that would study planets forming from that material. A Shanghai-led team has proposed flying 901 meter telescopes in coordinated formation.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:55.260
The array would observe the same planetary transit simultaneously to reach the one part in a million signal required for atmospheric characterization.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:07.980
An Earth analog planet transits its star only once per year, making each event irreplaceable. The distributed design avoids the engineering challenge of a single, extremely large space mirror.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:19.220
Engineering studies are now assessing formation-flying precision requirements. Ambitious telescope arrays point to another ambitious goal using the moon itself as a resource base.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:32.400
Commercial teams are using NASA lunar data sets to design systems that would allow sustained operations on the moon. The data sets include surface composition maps, radiation measurements, and resource distribution information.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:44.020
Companies intend to process local regolith for construction materials and life support consumables. The approach supports broader objectives for long-term human presence beyond low Earth orbit.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:56.500
Additional studies will evaluate how directly the existing data sets translate into hardware specifications. From planning future lunar infrastructure, we shift to how the human body itself fares in space.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:10.360
Human heart muscle cells grown under microgravity conditions on the International Space Station maintained normal contraction strength. The result stands in contrast to the documented weakening of bone and skeletal muscle during space flight.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:22.020
Experiments relied on specialized culture systems designed for the station's environment. The findings suggest cardiac muscle may respond differently to the absence of gravity loading than other tissues.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:34.380
Longer duration studies are planned to determine whether extended missions produce measurable changes. From human physiology and orbit, we look at how Earth itself appears from space during another natural event.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:40.180
A deep partial lunar eclipse will occur on 27 to 28 August, 2026.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:50.420
Observers across much of the Americas in Europe will see the moon pass through Earth's shadow. The event marks the final lunar eclipse before a multi-year observational gap.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:58.440
Timing predictions and viewing guidance are already posted by the magazine. Updated weather forecasts will be issued closer to the date.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:10.920
From upcoming eclipse is visible from Earth, we close with images already capturing recent ones. Today's astronomy picture of the day presents an image of the 12th August total solar eclipse as seen from Spain.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:20.600
The photograph captures the solar corona and the darkened landscape during totality. A brief explanation written by a professional astronomer accompanies the image.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:38.380
Archives of prior eclipse APODs are linked for direct comparison. The feature remains available on the NASA site. Keep an eye on the MTG-I-2 launch scheduled for August 27, 2026, as it moves the European weather satellite series toward full operational coverage.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:44.960
That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting.

